NAME
    vim — Open a file in the built-in terminal text editor.

SYNOPSIS
    vim <file>

DESCRIPTION
    The 'vim' command opens a file using the built-in terminal text editor.
    It behaves exactly like the 'edit' command and provides a familiar
    command name for users who prefer the traditional vim command.

    This is not actually Vim. No modal editing, no mysterious commands,
    and hopefully no accidental escape-room experience.

ARGUMENTS
    file    The file to open in the terminal editor.
    Can be a relative or absolute path.

USAGE
    > vim file.txt
        Opens file.txt in the built-in terminal editor.
    
EXAMPLES
    > vim README.txt
        Opens README.txt for editing.
    
    > vim config.ini
        Opens config.ini in the terminal editor.

FUN FACTS
    - vim is an alias for edit.
    - The command uses the same built-in TerminalEditor.
    - You do not need to install Vim inside the container.
    - Your muscle memory may still try to type :wq.

